/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:10:It has since gained a lot of attention and has been well-studied from both a graph-theoretic perspective, e.g.,~\cite{AlvaradoDMR19,BianchiNTT21,CutlerR16,SuzukiMN16,Taletskii22}, and an algorithmic perspective, e.g.,~\cite{eisenbrand2004complexity,PatrascuW10,FominLST12,FominT03,SLM18,SolomonU23}.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:12:Besides being one of the classic NP-complete problems, the $k$-Dominating Set problem has proven valuable within the realm of parameterized complexity theory, where it is regarded as perhaps the most natural $W[2]$-complete problem~\cite{DowneyF95}, as well as fine-grained complexity in P, where it was among first problems for which tight lower bounds under the Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (SETH) have been established~\cite{PatrascuW10}.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:17:We are given a graph $G$ and an integer $k$ and want to decide if there exists a set of vertices $S=\{x_1,\dots, x_k\}$ that satisfies some fixed inner property $\phi(x_1,\dots, x_k)$ such that for every vertex $v\in V(G)$ the \emph{domination} property $\rho(x_1,\dots, x_k, v)$ is satisfied.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:21:    \item $x_1, \dots, x_k$ form an independent set (Independent Domination).
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:31:Many of these domination problems have not seen any polynomial improvements over brute force in dense graphs, i.e., the best known algorithms for finding a solution of size~$k$ typically run in $\Omega(n^{k})$ time and for some variants it has been shown that improving upon these algorithms significantly would refute some of the popular fine-grained complexity assumptions.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:32:Most notably, Pătraşcu and Williams~\cite{PatrascuW10} show that an $\bigO(n^{k-\epsilon})$ algorithm solving $k$-Dominating Set, for any~\makebox{$k\ge 3$} and $\epsilon > 0$ would refute the Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (SETH).
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:36:Recently, Fischer, Künnemann and Redzic~\cite{fischer2024effect} proved that the fine-grained complexity of $k$-Dominating set shows a non-trivial sensitivity to sparsity of the input graph. 
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:42:For a graph $G=(V,E)$ we say a subset of vertices $S$ is an \emph{$r$-multiple dominating set} if each vertex $v\in V\setminus S$ has at least $r$ neighbours in $S$.\footnote{We remark that in the literature, this concept is better known under the name \emph{$r$-Dominating Set}. In the setting of parameterized complexity, however, the notion of $k$-Dominating Set usually refers to dominating sets of size $k$, so for clarity, we use the term \emph{$r$-Multiple Dominating Set}.}
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:43:Given a graph $G$ with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges, the \emph{$r$-Multiple $k$-Dominating Set} problem is to decide if there is an $r$-multiple dominating set $S$ of size at most $k$.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:45:We note that all of the algorithms and lower bounds that we provide for $r$-Multiple $k$-Dominating Set work with very minor modifications for $r$-Tuple Dominating Set as well.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:49:In particular, we distinguish between the two cases, $r\leq k-2$ and $r=k-1$ (note that if $r\geq k$, the problem becomes trivial) -- it turns out that the fine-grained complexity differs already in the dense case for these two cases.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:51:Let us begin with a baseline algorithm for the case $r\leq k-2$: 
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:52:%Namely, in the former case, we show that solving this problem significantly faster than brute-force would refute the $3$-Uniform $k$-Hyperclique Hypothesis (in fact, for $k=3$, as P{\u{a}}tra{\c{s}}cu and Williams show in~\cite{PatrascuW10}, such an algorithm would even refute Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis) [see Section \ref{section:lower-bound} for details].
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:54:%The case $k=3$ is settled in~\cite{fischer2024effect} with an affirmative answer, providing an algorithm running in $mn^{1+o(1)}$ and a matching SETH conditional lower bound. 
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:55:%We advance this line of research and settle this question in general for all $k$ affirmatively by modifying the baseline algorithm from~\cite{fischer2024effect} to obtain significant improvements in sparse graphs, and proving the following theorem.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:56:\begin{theorem}\label{th:rleqk-2:algorithm-simple}
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:57:Let $k\geq 3$ and $r\leq k-2$ be fixed constants. Given a graph $G$ with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges we can solve $r$-Multiple $k$-Dominating Set in time $(m/n)^r n^{k-r+o(1)}$, assuming $\omega = 2$.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:59:We note the remarkable improvement by a factor of $\Theta(\frac{n^{2r}}{m^r})$ over the best known algorithm in dense graphs.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:60:Already for Double $k$-Dominating Set (i.e., $r=2$), this yields an algorithm running in $m^2n^{k-4 + o(1)}$, which beats the running time of the best $k$-Dominating Set algorithm \cite{fischer2024effect} by a factor of $\Theta(n^2/m)$.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:61:Even better, if we want each vertex in our graph to be dominated by precisely $50\%$ of the solution vertices, we get an algorithm running in $m^{\frac{k}{2}+o(1)}$, halving the exponent in sparse graphs.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:62:Perhaps surprisingly, for $r$ close to $k$, this yields an algorithm whose running time exponent is independent of $k$ when the input graph is very sparse.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:64:%We note the remarkable improvement by a factor of $\Theta(\frac{n^{2r}}{m^r})$ over the best known algorithm in the dense case. This improvement, perhaps surprisingly, yields an algorithm polynomially faster than $m^{\frac{k}{2}}$ when $r>\frac{k}{2}$, which is a typical target to aim for in the sparse graphs.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:66:We answer this question negatively, and in fact show that any polynomial improvement over our algorithm (up to resolving the matrix multiplication exponent $\omega$) would refute the $3$-uniform Hyperclique Hypothesis\footnote{For a definition of the $3$-uniform Hyperclique Hypothesis, we refer to Section \ref{app-hardness}.}, thus settling the complexity of this problem in sparse graphs whenever $r\leq k-2$.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:67:\begin{restatable}{theorem}{TheoremrSmallLB}\label{th:rleqk-2:lb}
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:68:    Let $k\geq 3$, $r\leq k-2$ be fixed constants, and $\varepsilon>0$.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:69:    An algorithm solving $r$-Multiple $k$-Dominating Set in time $O((m/n)^{r}n^{k-r-\varepsilon})$ would refute $3$-uniform Hyperclique Hypothesis. This holds even when restricting $m=\Theta(n^{1+\gamma})$ for any $0< \gamma \le 1$.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:71:While the algorithmic approach of Theorem~\ref{th:rleqk-2:algorithm-simple} is applicable also for the remaining case of $r=k-1$, it turns out that the resulting upper bound of $(m/n)^{k-1} n^{1+o(1)}$ (if $\omega=2$), is not optimal in general: In fact, we reduce the problem to Clique detection, by observing that each pair of solution vertices dominates the whole graph (i.e., forms a dominating set of size 2). The resulting algorithm substantially improves over exhaustive search already in dense graphs.  
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:72:%On the other hand, for the remaining case of $r=k-1$, we implicitly show that even when the input graph is dense, this problem admits an algorithm faster than brute-force and is in fact
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:74:%equivalent to the $k$-Clique Detection. 
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:75:%Still, in sparse graphs, the algorithm from \autoref{th:rleqk-2:algorithm}, combined with the Bloom-Filter inspired hashing technique introduced by Fischer, Künnemann and Redzic in~\cite{fischer2024effect} gives us a non-trivial improvement over this algorithm for sufficiently large $k$.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:76:%Harary and Haynes \mirza{Actually 1985 Fink and Jacobson, Harary and Haynes introduced $r$-Tuple Domination (a distinction similar to reflexive/irreflexive settings)}~\cite{HARARY199699} and has been intensively studied since (see e.g.~\cite{Araki08,Araki09,argiroffo2018complexity,argiroffo2015complexity,GagarinZ08,HenningK10,LiWS23}). A subset $D\subseteq V$ is said to be $r$-tuple dominating if the closed neighborhood of every vertex $v\in V$ intersects with $D$ in at least $r$ elements. In other words, each vertex is dominated by at least $r$ elements of $D$.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:77:%Furthermore, in sparse graphs, we can do even better by leveraging a simple observation that each pair of vertices in any solution set dominates the whole graph (i.e. forms a dominating set of size $2$).
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:78:Furthermore, in sparse graphs, we can apply a Bloom-filter inspired randomized algorithm of~\cite{fischer2024effect}, allowing us to list all 2-dominating sets efficiently, to obtain an efficient randomized reduction to an Unbalanced $k$-Clique Detection instance with $k-1$ parts of size $\bigO(\frac{m}{n})$ and one of size $n$, which we denote as $k$-Clique($\frac{m}{n},\dots, \frac{m}{n}, n$).\footnote{For more details, we refer to Section~\ref{sec:prelims} and Section~\ref{sec:multiple-kds}.}
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:79:\begin{restatable}{theorem}{TheoremReductionToUnbalancedClique}\label{theorem:reduction-to-unbalanced-clique}
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:80:    For any fixed constant $k\geq 2$ let $T_k(m, n)$ denote the time required to solve the $k$-Clique($\frac{m}{n},\dots, \frac{m}{n}, n$) problem. There is a randomized algorithm solving $(k-1)$-Multiple $k$-Dominating Set in time $m^{\frac{\omega}{2}+o(1)} + \bigO(T_k(m,n))$.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:82:It remains to analyze the complexity of the Unbalanced Clique problem.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:83:It is straightforward to obtain an algorithm solving this problem in time $\frac{m^2}{n} \cdot \big(\frac{m}{n}\big)^{\omega k/3+o(1)}$, which in case of very sparse graphs ($m=\tilde \bigO(n)$) yields a near-linear running time.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:85:More precisely, we show that for each positive integer $p$, we can solve $k$-Clique($n^{\frac{1}{p}}, \dots, n^{\frac{1}{p}}, n$) in time $(n^{\frac{1}{p}(k-1)+1})^{\frac{\omega}{3}+o(1)}$ for all sufficiently large $k$ satisfying $k\equiv 2p+1 \pmod 3$.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:95:Applying this running time to our setting, this yields an algorithm solving $(k-1)$-Multiple $k$-Dominating Set in the applicable cases in time $\big(n(\frac{m}{n})^{k-1}\big)^{\omega/3 + o(1)}$.
/tmp/tmp.bEoUxnvCNB/src/introduction.tex:97:We also complement this with a matching conditional lower bound based on the $k$-Clique Hypothesis for the $(k-1)$-Multiple $k$-Dominating Set problem.
