/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/main.tex:69:%    \item In \emph{$r$-Multiple Dominating Set}, we require each vertex in $V(G)$ to be adjacent to at least $r$ of the $k$ solution vertices. We consider the version of this problem parameterized by both $r$ and $k$. In such a setting, two natural regimes arise, $r\leq k-2$ and $r=k-1$. In the former regime, we demonstrate that under $3$-Uniform Hyperclique Hypothesis, the exhaustive search running time $n^{k\pm o(1)}$ is the best we can achieve. 
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/main.tex:70:%    When the input graph is sparse, however, we can obtain a significant polynomial improvement and a running time of $m^{r}n^{k-2r+o(1)}$ and prove that this running time cannot be improved unless $3$-Uniform Hyperclique Hypothesis fails. 
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/main.tex:71:%    For the latter regime, we demonstrate a randomized algorithm solving this problem in "$k$-clique" running time ($n^{\omega k/3+o(1)}$), and we show that any significant improvement over this running time would refute the $k$-Clique hypothesis.
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/main.tex:73:%    For the first two, we provide an algorithm running in time $\frac{m^{1+(k-1)/2+o(1)}}{n}$ and show that any polynomial improvement over this running time would refute $k$-OV Hypothesis. Finally, for the Dominating $k$-Perfect Matching we obtain an algorithm running in $m^{k/2+o(1)}$ and complement this by a matching $k$-OV based conditional lower bound.
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/main.tex:77:The study of domination in graphs has led to a variety of \emph{dominating set} problems studied in the literature. Most of these follow the following general framework: Given a graph $G$ and an integer~$k$, decide if there is a set $S$ of $k$ vertices such that (1) some inner connectivity property $\phi(S)$ (e.g., connectedness) is satisfied, and (2) each vertex $v$ satisfies some domination property $\rho(S, v)$ (e.g., there is some $s\in S$ that is adjacent to $v$).
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/main.tex:79:Since many real-world graphs are \emph{sparse}, we seek to determine the optimal running time of such problems in both the number $n$ of vertices and the number $m$ of edges in $G$. While the classic dominating set problem admits a rather  limited improvement in sparse graphs (Fischer, Künnemann, Redzic SODA'24), we show that natural variants studied in the literature admit much larger speed-ups, with a diverse set of possible running times. Specifically, if the matrix exponent $\omega$ equals $2$, we obtain conditionally optimal algorithms for:   
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/main.tex:81: \item \emph{$r$-Multiple $k$-Dominating Set} (each vertex $v$ must be adjacent to at least $r$ vertices in $S$): If $r\le k-2$, we obtain a running time of $(m/n)^{r} n^{k-r+o(1)}$ that is conditionally optimal assuming the 3-uniform hyperclique hypothesis. In sparse graphs, this fully interpolates between $n^{k-1\pm o(1)}$ and $n^{2\pm o(1)}$, depending on $r$. Curiously, when $r=k-1$, we obtain a randomized algorithm beating $(m/n)^{k-1} n^{1+o(1)}$ and we show that this algorithm is close to optimal under the $k$-clique hypothesis.
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/main.tex:82: \item \emph{$H$-Dominating Set} ($S$ must induce a pattern $H$). We conditionally settle the complexity of three such problems: (a) Dominating Clique ($H$ is a $k$-clique), (b) Maximal Independent Set of size~$k$ ($H$ is an independent set on $k$ vertices), (c) Dominating Induced Matching ($H$ is a perfect matching on $k$ vertices). For all sufficiently large $k$, we provide algorithms with running time $(m/n)m^{(k-1)/2+o(1)}$ for (a) and (b), and $m^{k/2+o(1)}$ for (c). We show that these algorithms are essentially optimal under the $k$-Orthogonal Vectors Hypothesis ($k$-OVH). This is in contrast to $H$ being the $k$-Star, which is susceptible only to a very limited improvement, with the best algorithm running in time $n^{k-1 \pm o(1)}$ in sparse graphs under $k$-OVH. 
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/introduction.tex:3:\section{Introduction}
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/introduction.tex:10:It has since gained a lot of attention and has been well-studied from both a graph-theoretic perspective, e.g.,~\cite{AlvaradoDMR19,BianchiNTT21,CutlerR16,SuzukiMN16,Taletskii22}, and an algorithmic perspective, e.g.,~\cite{eisenbrand2004complexity,PatrascuW10,FominLST12,FominT03,SLM18,SolomonU23}.
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/introduction.tex:31:Many of these domination problems have not seen any polynomial improvements over brute force in dense graphs, i.e., the best known algorithms for finding a solution of size~$k$ typically run in $\Omega(n^{k})$ time and for some variants it has been shown that improving upon these algorithms significantly would refute some of the popular fine-grained complexity assumptions.
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/introduction.tex:32:Most notably, Pătraşcu and Williams~\cite{PatrascuW10} show that an $\bigO(n^{k-\epsilon})$ algorithm solving $k$-Dominating Set, for any~\makebox{$k\ge 3$} and $\epsilon > 0$ would refute the Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (SETH).
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/introduction.tex:35:Hence, it is natural to ask what is the best running time of domination problems in sparse graphs.
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/introduction.tex:36:Recently, Fischer, Künnemann and Redzic~\cite{fischer2024effect} proved that the fine-grained complexity of $k$-Dominating set shows a non-trivial sensitivity to sparsity of the input graph. 
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/introduction.tex:37:More precisely, despite the SETH-based lower bound of $n^{k-o(1)}$, they prove that when the input graph is sufficiently sparse, we can in fact improve upon this running time significantly by using sparse matrix multiplication techniques, and obtain a conditionally optimal running time of $mn^{k-2+o(1)}$ for all $k\ge 2$ (assuming $\omega=2$).
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/introduction.tex:41:\subparagraph{Multiple Domination in Graphs.} The concept of Multiple Domination has been introduced as a generalization of Dominating Set by Fink and Jacobson in 1985~\cite{fink1985n,finkj1985n} and has been intensively studied since (see e.g.~\cite{Araki08,Araki09,argiroffo2018complexity,argiroffo2015complexity,GagarinZ08,HenningK10,LiWS23}). 
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/introduction.tex:42:For a graph $G=(V,E)$ we say a subset of vertices $S$ is an \emph{$r$-multiple dominating set} if each vertex $v\in V\setminus S$ has at least $r$ neighbours in $S$.\footnote{We remark that in the literature, this concept is better known under the name \emph{$r$-Dominating Set}. In the setting of parameterized complexity, however, the notion of $k$-Dominating Set usually refers to dominating sets of size $k$, so for clarity, we use the term \emph{$r$-Multiple Dominating Set}.}
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/introduction.tex:43:Given a graph $G$ with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges, the \emph{$r$-Multiple $k$-Dominating Set} problem is to decide if there is an $r$-multiple dominating set $S$ of size at most $k$.
/tmp/paper2409.OkTdSz/introduction.tex:45:We note that all of the algorithms and lower bounds that we provide for $r$-Multiple $k$-Dominating Set work with very minor modifications for $r$-Tuple Dominating Set as well.
