     2	\section{Dominating Patterns in Sparse Graphs} 
     3	In this section we consider the Pattern Domination problem.
     4	In particular, we first provide a simple argument that shows that for every pattern $H$ consisting of $k$ vertices (for $k\geq 3$), we can solve this problem in $mn^{k-2+o(1)}$ running time. % by a simple modification of the $k$-Dominating Set algorithm in sparse graphs from \cite{fischer2024effect}.
     5	On the lower bound side, we observe that the literature implicitly proves existence of a pattern $H$ for which this running time is optimal under the $k$-OV Hypothesis, thus settling the complexity of the case when the pattern $H$ is a part of the input.
     6	We then consider the problem of detecting an $H$-Dominating Set for a fixed $k$-vertex graph $H$.
     7	To this end, we show that for any fixed pattern $H$ consisting of $k$ vertices, the existence of an $O(\frac{m^{(k-1)/2+1-\varepsilon}}{n})$-time algorithm for this problem would refute the $k$-OV Hypothesis. We then show that this general lower bound is matched by a corresponding algorithm for some patterns $H$. 
     8	The fine-grained complexity thus depends heavily on the structure of the graph $H$ itself, and we focus our attention to some of the most important patterns.
     9	
    10	
    11	%We then settle the complexity of three well-studied patterns, namely $k$-Clique, $k$-Independent Set and the Perfect Matching of size $k$.
    12	%For each of the first two, we provide an algorithm running in $\frac{m^{k/2+1}}{n}$, thus matching our lower bound for a general pattern. 
    13	%For the Perfect Matching of size $k$, we first give a simple algorithm running in $m^{k/2}$ and then complement this by providing a tight conditional lower bound based on $k$-OV Hypothesis.
    14	\begin{restatable}{proposition}{PropkPatternDomAlg}
    15	Let $k\ge 7$. The $k$-Pattern Domination on graphs with~$n$ vertices and $m$ edges can be solved in time $O(mn^{k-2 + o(1)})$ (if $\omega = 2$, we can achieve this running time for all $k\geq 3$).
