    32	\subsection{Dominating $k$-Clique and $k$-Independent Set}
    33	In this section we consider the two classic graph patterns for which this problem has been well-studied, namely, $k$-Clique and $k$-Independent Set. 
    34	Particularly, we settle the fine-grained complexity of both Dominating $k$-Clique and Dominating $k$-Independent Set by providing algorithms that match the conditional lower bound from \autoref{prop:h-dom-lb}.
    35	Let us focus on the $k$-Clique case first.
    36	In order to obtain a faster algorithm in sparse graphs, we leverage the following observation (for a proof see Appendix \ref{appendix-patt-dom}).
    37	\begin{restatable}{observation}{ObsNumberOfkCliquesInGraph}[folklore]\label{obs:k-clique-number}
    38	    A graph with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges has at most $\bigO(m^{\frac{k}{2}})$ $k$-cliques.
    39	\end{restatable}
    40	{\renewcommand\footnote[1]{}\cliqueDomAlg*}
    41	The idea of the proof is to combine \autoref{obs:k-clique-number} with the observation that each dominating $k$-clique contains a heavy vertex, in order to restrict the size of our solution space.
    42	After restricting the solution space, we follow the similar lines of the matrix multiplication algorithm for $k$-Dominating Set from \cite{eisenbrand2004complexity,fischer2024effect}.
    43	For the full proof, see Appendix \ref{appendix-patt-dom}, Section \ref{app-section:clique}.
    44	
    45	The last theorem shows that considering the density of the dominating pattern can be beneficial in obtaining a significant speedup over the standard $k$-Dominating Set algorithm, by observing that there are fewer such dense patterns (e.g. $k$-cliques) in sparse graphs.
    46	On the other extreme of the density spectrum lie the independent sets.
    47	There are typically many $k$-independent sets in sparse graphs ($\Omega(n^k)$), so we cannot use \autoref{obs:k-clique-number} to obtain a faster algorithm for Dominating $k$-Independent Set problem.
    48	To nevertheless obtain a fast algorithm, we take advantage of one simple observation.
    49	Namely, if we know that some fixed vertex $v$ is contained in some dominating $k$-independent set, by removing $N[v]$ from $G$, we can recursively obtain an instance of Dominating $(k-1)$-Independent Set problem, since no solution vertices will appear in $N[v]$ and moreover, $N[v]$ is already dominated by $v$.
    50	As a technical note, the crucial reason why this approach fails for instances of the usual $k$-Dominating Set problem (without the restriction that the solution vertices induce a $k$-Independent Set) lies in the distinction between monochromatic and bichromatic versions of Dominating Set. 
    51	In particular, after fixing a solution vertex $v$ of a dominating set, we no longer have to dominate the vertices from $N[v]$, but some of them might still appear in our solution.
    52	We thus obtain an instance of Bichromatic $(k-1)$-Dominating Set (essentially a graph formulation of $k$-Set Cover), and it is known that this problem is hard already in very sparse instances (see, e.g.,~\cite{fischer2024effect}).
    53	\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:domindset-recursion}
    54	    Let $A_k(G)$ be an algorithm that finds a dominating $k$-independent set.
    55	    Given a graph $G$, any dominating $k$-independent set containing a fixed vertex $v$ can be found by running $A_{k-1}(G-N[v])$.
    56	\end{lemma}
    57	%\begin{proof}
    58	%    Let $S:=\{x_1,\dots, x_{k-1}, v\}$ be any dominating $k$-independent set. 
    59	%    Since $S$ is an independent set, no vertex $x_i$ is contained in $N[v]$.
    60	%    Moreover, since $S$ is a dominating set, each vertex in $G-N[v]$ is dominated by at least one $x_i$. 
    61	%\end{proof}
    62	This gives rise to a simple recursive algorithm whose time complexity we can bound as follows. (For a formal proof, we refer to Section~\ref{app-section:indset}.)
    63	\begin{restatable}{lemma}{LemmaIndSetDomTC}
    64	    Let $T_k(n,m)$ denote the running time of an algorithm solving Dominating $k$-Independent Set problem and $\mathcal{H}$ denote the set of heavy vertices.
    65	    Then for every $k\geq 3$, the following inequality holds: 
    66	    \[T_k(n,m) \leq \sum_{v\in \mathcal{H}}T_{k-1}(n - |N[v]|,m).\]
    67	\end{restatable}
    68	As the base case of our algorithm, we take the $k=2$ case, which we can solve in randomized time $m^{\omega/2 + o(1)}$.
    69	\begin{lemma} \label{lemma:2ids-alg}
    70	    There exists a randomized algorithm solving Dominating $2$-Independent Set in time $m^{\omega/2 + o(1)}$.
    71	\end{lemma}
    72	\begin{proof}
    73	    By \autoref{lemma:2ds-listing}, we can list all dominating sets of size $2$ in time $m^{\omega/2 + o(1)}$ and for each we can in $\bigO(1)$ time check if it forms an independent set.
    74	\end{proof}
    75	We can now give a full algorithm with the analysis by exploiting the previous lemmas.
    76	\indsetdomalg*
    77	\begin{proof}
    78	    %Combining the previous lemmas, we can construct the following algorithm.
    79	    If $k=2$ we apply \autoref{lemma:2ids-alg} to solve the problem in $m^{\omega/2+o(1)}$.
    80	    For larger $k$, for each heavy vertex $v$, we ask if $G-N[v]$ contains an independent set of size $(k-1)$ that dominates $G$.
    81	    If for any choice of $v$ the recursive algorithm returns YES, we return YES and otherwise return NO.
    82	    We only have to analyse the time complexity.
    83	    We know by the previous lemma that $T_2(n,m) = m^{\omega/2 + o(1)}$.
    84	    %Let $1\leq \gamma\leq 2$ be the value of $\log_n(m)$, in other words $m = n^\gamma$.
    85	    For larger values of $k$, we have
    86	    \begin{align*}
    87	        T_k(n,m) & \leq \sum_{v\in \mathcal{H}}T_{k-1}(n - |N[v]|,m)\\
    88	                 & \leq \min(n,\frac{m}{n})\cdot \max_{v\in \mathcal{H}}T_{k-1}(n - |N[v]|,m)\\
    89	                 &\leq \frac{m}{n}\cdot \max_{\delta \in [0,1]}T_{k-1}(n^\delta,m)
    90	    \end{align*}
    91	    Now it only remains to bound the value $\max_{\delta \in [0,1]}T_{k-1}(n^\delta,m)$.
    92	    If $k\leq 3$, this value is bounded by $m^{\omega/2+o(1)}$ and we obtain the claimed running time. 
    93	    So assume that $k\geq 4$ and consider two separate cases, namely when $n^\delta<\sqrt{m}$ and when $n^\delta\geq \sqrt{m}$.
    94	    In the former case, we can simply list all dominating sets of size $k-1$ in time $n^{k-1 + o(1)}<m^{\frac{k-1}{2} + o(1)}$ (assuming $\omega = 2$) using the algorithm from \cite{PatrascuW10}\footnote{If $k\geq 8$, we can obtain this running time, even with the current value of $\omega$.}, and this again yields a running time of $(\frac{m^{(k-1 + \omega)/2}}{n})^{1+o(1)}$.
    95	    In the latter case we can proceed inductively, since $\frac{m}{n^\delta}\leq \sqrt{m}$, we have that $T_{k-1}(n^\delta,m) \leq \sqrt{m}\max_{\delta' \in [0,1]}T_{k-2}(n^{\delta'},m)$ and we can bound $\max_{\delta' \in [0,1]}T_{k-2}(n^{\delta'},m)\leq m^{(k-2)/2}$ by a simple induction on $k$, yielding the desired running time.
    96	\end{proof}
