   101	\subsection{Dominating $k$-Induced Matching}
   102	%In this section we show that Paired $k$-Dominating Set can be solved efficiently in sparse graphs.
   103	%We begin by providing a simple baseline algorithm for $k\geq 4$, we then provide a simple and efficient reduction from Paired $2$-Dominating Set to $2$-Dominating Set that allows us to exploit the efficient randomized algorithm from \cite{fischer2024effect} to solve this problem as well.
   104	%We also note prove that the algorithms that we provide can be simply modified to solve some other domination problems (most notably $k$-Clique Dominating Set problem).
   105	%We finally show that our algorithms for this problem are optimal under $k$-OV-Hypothesis.
   106	So far we considered three different pattern classes (cliques, independent sets and stars), and in two out of those three cases we can obtain an algorithm that runs in $\big (\frac{m^{(k+1)/2}}{n}\big)^{1+o(1)}$ (if $\omega = 2$), which is the best we can do for any pattern assuming $k$-OV Hypothesis, and in the remaining case we can show an $mn^{k-2-o(1)}$ conditional lower bound, which makes this pattern as hard as any pattern can be.
   107	This suggests that there might be a dichotomy of all $k$-vertex graphs into two classes:
   108	\begin{enumerate}
   109	    \item Easy Patterns (those for which there exists an algorithm solving $H$-Dominating Set in $\big (\frac{m^{(k+1)/2}}{n}\big)^{1+o(1)}$)
   110	    \item Hard Patterns (those for which we can show an $mn^{k-2-o(1)}$ lower bound under $k$-OV Hypothesis).
   111	\end{enumerate}
   112	In this section we show that such a dichotomy is unlikely.
   113	More precisely, we find a pattern which is in neither of those two categories (unless $k$-OV Hypothesis fails).
   114	Let $k$-induced matching be a graph consisting of $k/2$ independent edges.
   115	In this section we prove that we can solve the Dominating $k$-Induced Matching problem in $m^{k/2 + o(1)}$ running time (if $\omega=2$) and provide a matching conditional lower bound under $k$-OV Hypothesis.
   116	Due to lack of space, we only state our results; the full proofs can be found in Appendix \ref{appendix-patt-dom}.
   117	\begin{restatable}{theorem}{ThInducedMatchingAlgorithm}
   118	    Given a graph $G$ with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges, we can solve Dominating $k$-Induced Matching in time 
   119	    \[\
   120	    \MM(m^{\lceil\frac{k}{2}\rceil}, n, m^{\lfloor \frac{k}{2}\rfloor}).
   121	    \]
   122	    If $\omega=2$, this running time becomes $m^{\frac{k}{2}+o(1)}$ for every even $k\geq 4$.
   123	\end{restatable}
   124	\begin{comment}
   125	Notably, this algorithm does not give us any improvement over brute-force for the remaining case of $k=2$.
   126	In the remaining of this section, we provide an algorithm that handles this case by constructing an efficient reduction to $2$-Dominating Set and then using the randomized algorithm from \cite{fischer2024effect}.
   127	\begin{lemma}
   128	    Given a graph $G$ with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges, we can construct a graph $G'$ with $\bigO(n)$ vertices and $\bigO(m)$ edges such that $G$ has a Paired Dominating Set of size $2$ if and only if $G'$ has a Dominating Set of size $2$.
   129	\end{lemma}
   130	\begin{proof}
   131	    Given a graph $G = (V,E)$, let $G' = (V\cup V', E\cup E')$, where $V'$ is a copy of $V$ (for each $x\in V$ there is a unique $x'\in V'$ that corresponds to $x$).
   132	    Furthermore, each edge in $E'$ has one endpoint in $V$ and one in $V'$. In particular, for each pair $x,y \in V$, let $x'\in V'$ be the (unique) vertex corresponding to $x$. Then $(x',y)\in E'$ if and only if $(x,y)\in E'$.
   133	    In other words, if $x,x'$ are the corresponding pair, then $N(x') = N(x)\cap V$.
   134	    Notice that the graph $G'$ has exactly $2n$ vertices and $3m$ edges.
   135	    
   136	    Assume now that $x,y$ is a dominating set in $G'$.
   137	    We first consider the simplest case when $x\in V$, $y\in V'$ such that $y=x'$.
   138	    It is then easy to verify that the pair $x,w$ for any $w\in N(x)\cap V$ forms a Paired Dominating Set of size $2$.
   139	
   140	    Otherwise, it is sufficient to prove that $x$ and $y$ are adjacent in $G'$, since then they are also adjacent in $G$ (up to replacing one or the other by their corresponding vertices in $V$).
   141	    Assume for contradiction that this is not the case and consider the vertex $x'$ corresponding to $x$ (notice that here we can have either $x\in V, x'\in V'$ or vice versa). 
   142	    By construction, $x'$ is not adjacent to $x$ and since $x$ and $y$ are non-adjacent, it is also not adjacent to $y$, and hence not dominated by $x,y$, contradicting the assumption that $x,y$ dominates $G'$.
   143	
   144	    Conversely, we claim that if $x,y$ form a paired dominating set in $G$, then it also forms a dominating set in $G'$. 
   145	    Clearly, $x,y$ dominates all vertices in $V$, so it remains to show that all vertices in $v'$ are also dominated.
   146	    To this end, notice that for any dominating set $\{x,y\}\subseteq V$ it holds that $V'\setminus\{x',y'\} \subseteq N(x)\cup N(y)$.
   147	    Hence, any $v'\in V'\setminus \{x',y'\}$ is dominated.
   148	    Finally, if $v' = x'$, it is dominated by $y$ and similarly if $v'=y'$, it is dominated by $x$.
   149	\end{proof}
   150	\begin{theorem}
   151	    There exists a randomized algorithm solving Paired $2$-Dominating Set in time $m^{\frac{\omega}{2}+o(1)}$.
   152	\end{theorem}
   153	\begin{proof}
   154	    Using the previous lemma, reduce the paired $2$-Dominating Set to an equivalent instance of $2$-Dominating Set in linear time and apply the randomized algorithm from \cite{fischer2024effect}.
   155	\end{proof}
   156	We remark that all the algorithms and the running times from this section can be used in a slightly more general framework.
   157	Consider the following problem.
   158	\begin{definition}[Pattern $k$-Dominating Set]
   159	    Given a graph $G$ with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges and a graph $H$ on $k$ vertices, \emph{Pattern $k$-Dominating Set} problem is to decide if there is a set $S = \{x_1,\dots, x_k\}$ that dominates $G$ such that $G[S]$ is isomorphic to $H$. 
   160	\end{definition}
   161	\begin{corollary}
   162	    Given a graph $G$ with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges and a graph $H$ on $k$ vertices. 
   163	    Let $T_k(m,n)$ denote the running time of the Paired $k$-Dominating Set provided in this section. 
   164	    If $H$ admits a perfect matching, we can solve Pattern $k$-Dominating Set on $G$ in time $\bigO(T_k(m,n))$.
   165	\end{corollary}
   166	Note that many well-studied domination variants represent a special case of Pattern $k$-Dominating Set for a fixed graph $H$ (e.g. $k$-Induced Matching Dominating Set, $k$-Clique Dominating Set, $k$-Independent Dominating Set, etc.), and due to the previous corollary, we can solve any such \emph{$H$-Pattern $k$-Dominating Set} problem in the claimed time, as long as $H$ admits a perfect matching. 
   167	Most notably, this gives us a tool to efficiently solve \emph{Clique $k$-Dominating Set} for any even $k$ in sparse graphs \footnote{For odd $k\geq 5$, we remark that only additional overhead of $\frac{m}{n}$ suffices, by observing that there is a heavy vertex in any such dominating set and that the remaining vertices form a $(k-1)$-clique.}.
   168	\subsection{A Matching Conditional Lower Bound}
   169	In this section, the goal is prove the following Theorem and thus obtain a conditional lower bound that matches the running time of the algorithm in the previous section.
   170	\end{comment}
   171	Finally, we show that this running time cannot be significantly improved, unless $k$-OV Hypothesis fails.
   172	To achieve this, we apply a simple adaptation of the reduction from \autoref{prop:h-dom-lb}.
   173	\begin{restatable}{theorem}{ThInducedMatchingLB}
   174	    For no even $k\geq 4$ and $\varepsilon>0$ is there an algorithm solving Dominating $k$-Induced Matching in time $O(m^{\frac{k}{2}-\varepsilon})$, unless the $k$-OV hypothesis fails.
   175	\end{restatable}
