diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..caaa50555ad924333b4daac3cad2b412d10685ab
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,200 @@
+# arXiv 2409.08037 — cyclic multiple orthogonal vectors
+
+**Status: locally verified, but not yet shippable.** All deterministic gates and
+the G9 size/effort cap pass. The required OpenRouter runs could not make a
+single counted oracle attempt because the supplied key has no remaining limit;
+the script-owned transcripts contain only HTTP 403 errors. They are preserved
+as infrastructure evidence, not misreported as model failures.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| track | B — an efficient exact algorithm exists and is measured |
+| native domain | combinatorics |
+| object regime | finite discrete |
+| computational core | CSP/SAT |
+| certificate form | integer tuple of local vector-row indices |
+| intuition | invariant: parity quartets overlap on one hidden tight cycle |
+| domain essentiality | native |
+| reduction | none; this is the paper's explicitly defined intermediate problem |
+
+## Problem and trust boundary
+
+The source is Künnemann and Redzic,
+[*Fine-Grained Complexity of Multiple Domination and Dominating Patterns in
+Sparse Graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.08037). Section 3.2 defines
+`r`-Multiple `k`-Orthogonal Vectors: choose one binary vector from every set so
+that every coordinate contains at least `r` selected zeroes. This family uses
+`r=1`, which the paper notes is exactly `k`-OV.
+
+The generator samples a Boolean assignment first, builds a full-rank cyclic
+3-XOR system satisfied by it, replaces each XOR equation by its four exact
+3-CNF clauses, and partitions variables into complete assignment blocks. A
+block assignment is one binary vector, so the planted block rows have all-zero
+coordinatewise AND by construction. No generated instance is solved to obtain
+its witness. `verify` ignores `inst["answer"]`, checks row ranges, intersects
+the selected vectors as exact Python integers, and accepts iff the intersection
+is zero. Every block contains every assignment exactly once, so planted and
+decoy rows have the same distribution.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+Section 3.2's hardness lemma gives a full-product conditional lower bound for
+general `r`-Multiple `k`-OV when `r <= k-2`, even for unbalanced set sizes. That
+is a worst-case theorem, not evidence for this generated distribution. Section
+3.1 also supplies matrix-product algorithms for the corresponding multiple
+domination problem; the special `r=k-1` regime reduces further to clique, and
+`r=k` is trivial. Those facts rule out an honest Track-A claim here.
+
+For these generated instances, grouping clauses by their three variables
+recovers XOR rows. Generic Gauss–Jordan elimination over `GF(2)` solves 8/8
+shipping instances, with a measured maximum of 84,515 Boolean operations and
+0.014952 seconds per instance. The intended shortcut orders the rows through
+their two-variable overlaps and solves one cyclic recurrence. It succeeds 8/8
+in 288 exact bit operations. The benchmark tests recognition of that invariant,
+not resistance to software.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `make_instance(n=5, block_bits=2, copies=1, seed=3)`, the complete rendered
+instance is:
+
+```text
+Find a witness for this 1-Multiple k-Orthogonal Vectors instance.
+
+There are k ordered sets A_1,...,A_k of equal-length binary vectors.
+Choose exactly one vector from each set.  The choice is valid exactly
+when, in every coordinate, at least r=1 chosen vector has bit 0;
+equivalently, the coordinatewise Boolean AND of all chosen vectors is
+the all-zero vector.
+
+The vectors are specified exactly and compactly below.  Variables are
+Boolean.  A signed integer +j is variable x_j and -j is its negation;
+variables in clauses are 1-indexed.  Every clause is the OR of its three
+listed literals.  Each clause is one vector coordinate.  A row in A_i
+is a partial assignment to A_i's displayed variables, in that displayed
+order.  Its bit in a clause-coordinate is 0 iff one or more literals of
+that clause belonging to A_i is made true by the row; otherwise it is 1.
+Thus all vector bits are fixed by the data below, with no omitted edges,
+conditions, or conventions.
+
+Number of variables n: 5
+Number of vector sets k: 3
+Number of coordinates (indexed clauses): 20
+
+Vector sets.  Local row indices are zero-based.  A bit string gives the
+partial assignment in the exact variable order printed for that set:
+A_1 variables [4,1] | 0:11 1:10 2:01 3:00
+A_2 variables [2,3] | 0:10 1:11 2:01 3:00
+A_3 variables [5] | 0:0 1:1
+
+Coordinates, one signed JSON triple per line:
+c1: [2,-3,4]
+c2: [-5,4,-1]
+c3: [1,-5,2]
+c4: [-5,4,3]
+c5: [-2,-3,1]
+c6: [2,-3,-1]
+c7: [-4,5,-1]
+c8: [3,-4,5]
+c9: [-4,3,2]
+c10: [2,1,3]
+c11: [3,4,-2]
+c12: [-2,5,1]
+c13: [3,-2,-1]
+c14: [-2,-4,-3]
+c15: [5,2,-1]
+c16: [-5,-4,-3]
+c17: [-2,-1,-5]
+c18: [1,5,4]
+c19: [-3,4,5]
+c20: [1,-4,-5]
+
+Return a JSON list of exactly k local row indices, in A_1,...,A_k
+order.  The row for A_i must lie from 0 through |A_i|-1 inclusive.
+Row numbers are local, so repetitions across different sets are allowed;
+the order of the k output positions is mandatory.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as a JSON list of integers.
+Example format for three sets: <answer>[2,0,3]</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The answer is `<answer>[1,2,0]</answer>` and verifies as `(True, "ok")`.
+Dropping its last row gives `(False, "answer has too few row indices: expected
+3, got 2")`. A person can solve this five-variable illustration on paper by
+regrouping the 20 clauses into five parity quartets.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | variables | block bits | copies | sets | coordinates | answer atoms | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 5 | 2 | 1 | 3 | 20 | 3 | hand-solvable; hardener skips it |
+| easy | 23 | 4 | 1 | 6 | 92 | 6 | oracle unavailable before attempt 1 |
+| medium | 47 | 4 | 2 | 12 | 376 | 12 | locally verified; not reached by oracle |
+| **hard** | **95** | **4** | **3** | **24** | **1,140** | **24** | candidate shipping rung; oracle evidence pending |
+
+`SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` is provisionally `hard`. `escalate()` increases duplicate
+clause crowding while keeping the 24-element answer fixed; after eight copies
+it returns `cap_bound`.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | result | measured evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted witnesses verify and JSON-round-trip |
+| G2 | pass | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged prose/fence round-trip; garbage returns `None` |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; exact density `2^-95` |
+| G5 | pass | shipping exact count 1; 2,048 restarts fail in 0.152741 s; demo count 1/32 |
+| G6 | pass | four attacks each 0/8; reference and compact solvers each 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass | doubled `n=190` instance builds and verifies; fixed-answer escalation exists |
+| G8 | pass | 80/80 real relabellings invariant/valid; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 56 chars, 50 estimated tokens, 24 atoms, 288 operations |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+The harness made four redraws per run, but errors do not consume attempts. These
+tables deliberately show `0/0`, not `0/3`.
+
+| run | requested rung | counted solved/attempts | outcome |
+|---|---|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0/0 | four HTTP 403 key-limit errors; harness aborted |
+| structural hint | hard | 0/0 | four HTTP 403 key-limit errors; harness aborted |
+| placebo hint | hard | 0/0 | four HTTP 403 key-limit errors; harness aborted |
+
+Hinted minus placebo is undefined because neither arm has a counted attempt.
+No conclusion about model difficulty or the value of the hint is justified yet.
+The intended-route and answer-size caps do pass independently.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_2409_08037 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=19, **DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+assert verify(inst, inst["answer"]) == (True, "ok")
+prompt = render(inst)
+```
+
+After replenishing or replacing the OpenRouter key, rerun all three harness
+arms and then refresh `G9_ORACLE_RESULTS`, `selftest_report.json`, and this page.
+From the repository root, a completed result would emit with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2409.08037 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This distribution is easy with tools: parity-quartet recovery plus Gaussian
+elimination is complete. The uniform candidate prior in G4 already enforces one
+in-range row per set and is exactly uniform over all `2^95` assignments, but it
+does not model an XOR-aware solver. Duplicate clause copies increase prompt and
+vector-coordinate crowding without adding logical constraints.
+
+No industrial SAT/SMT solver or specialized `k`-OV meet-in-the-middle package
+was run; the exact Gauss–Jordan implementation is the domain-aware reference.
+The canonical key handles row, set, variable, clause, literal, cyclic-rotation,
+and reversal relabellings, but does not claim to solve arbitrary isomorphism of
+all possible vector-set instances. Most importantly, the four-vendor oracle
+claim is absent until the script-owned runs complete successfully.
