+The generator samples a Boolean assignment first, constructs a full-rank cyclic
+3-XOR system satisfied by it, replaces every XOR equation by its four exact CNF
+clauses, and partitions variables into complete assignment blocks. Each block
+assignment is one binary vector. Thus the planted rows have coordinatewise AND
+zero by composition of identities; no generated instance is solved to obtain
+its certificate. `verify` ignores `inst["answer"]`, range-checks the row indices,
+and intersects the selected vectors as exact integers. Every block contains all
+assignments, so planted and decoy rows have the same distribution.
 
 ## Why Track B
 
-Section 3.2's hardness lemma gives a full-product conditional lower bound for
-general `r`-Multiple `k`-OV when `r <= k-2`, even for unbalanced set sizes. That
-is a worst-case theorem, not evidence for this generated distribution. Section
-3.1 also supplies matrix-product algorithms for the corresponding multiple
-domination problem; the special `r=k-1` regime reduces further to clique, and
-`r=k` is trivial. Those facts rule out an honest Track-A claim here.
+Lemma 19 proves a conditional full-product lower bound for general fixed-`k`,
+`r <= k-2` instances, not for this structured distribution. The paper also
+identifies easy or faster regimes: Theorem 10 gives the matrix-product algorithm
+for `r <= k-2` domination, Theorem 3 handles `r=k-1` through unbalanced clique,
+and `r>=k` is trivial. The shipping construction therefore makes no Track-A
+claim.
 
