 ## Problem and trust boundary
 
 The source is Künnemann and Redzic,
 [*Fine-Grained Complexity of Multiple Domination and Dominating Patterns in
-Sparse Graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.08037). Section 3.2 defines
-`r`-Multiple `k`-Orthogonal Vectors: choose one binary vector from every set so
-that every coordinate contains at least `r` selected zeroes. This family uses
-`r=1`, which the paper notes is exactly `k`-OV.
+Sparse Graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.08037). Definition 16 in Section 3.2
+defines `r`-Multiple `k`-Orthogonal Vectors: select one binary vector from each
+set so that every coordinate has a zero in at least `r` selected vectors. This
+family uses `r=1`, exactly `k`-OV. Lemma 17 makes these objects central rather
+than a convenience surrogate by reducing them to sparse multiple-domination
+graphs.
 
-The generator samples a Boolean assignment first, builds a full-rank cyclic
-3-XOR system satisfied by it, replaces each XOR equation by its four exact
-3-CNF clauses, and partitions variables into complete assignment blocks. A
-block assignment is one binary vector, so the planted block rows have all-zero
-coordinatewise AND by construction. No generated instance is solved to obtain
-its witness. `verify` ignores `inst["answer"]`, checks row ranges, intersects
-the selected vectors as exact Python integers, and accepts iff the intersection
-is zero. Every block contains every assignment exactly once, so planted and
-decoy rows have the same distribution.
+The generator samples a Boolean assignment first, constructs a full-rank cyclic
+3-XOR system satisfied by it, replaces every XOR equation by its four exact CNF
+clauses, and partitions variables into complete assignment blocks. Each block
+assignment is one binary vector. Thus the planted rows have coordinatewise AND
+zero by composition of identities; no generated instance is solved to obtain
+its certificate. `verify` ignores `inst["answer"]`, range-checks the row indices,
+and intersects the selected vectors as exact integers. Every block contains all
+assignments, so planted and decoy rows have the same distribution.
 
 ## Why Track B
 
-Section 3.2's hardness lemma gives a full-product conditional lower bound for
-general `r`-Multiple `k`-OV when `r <= k-2`, even for unbalanced set sizes. That
-is a worst-case theorem, not evidence for this generated distribution. Section
-3.1 also supplies matrix-product algorithms for the corresponding multiple
-domination problem; the special `r=k-1` regime reduces further to clique, and
-`r=k` is trivial. Those facts rule out an honest Track-A claim here.
+Lemma 19 proves a conditional full-product lower bound for general fixed-`k`,
+`r <= k-2` instances, not for this structured distribution. The paper also
+identifies easy or faster regimes: Theorem 10 gives the matrix-product algorithm
+for `r <= k-2` domination, Theorem 3 handles `r=k-1` through unbalanced clique,
+and `r>=k` is trivial. The shipping construction therefore makes no Track-A
+claim.
 
-For these generated instances, grouping clauses by their three variables
-recovers XOR rows. Generic Gauss–Jordan elimination over `GF(2)` solves 8/8
-shipping instances, with a measured maximum of 93,727 Boolean operations and
-0.009466 seconds per instance on average. The intended shortcut orders the rows through
-their two-variable overlaps and solves one cyclic recurrence. It succeeds 8/8
-in 288 exact bit operations. The benchmark tests recognition of that invariant,
+For these instances, a standard exact route groups the parity quartets and runs
+Gauss–Jordan elimination over `GF(2)`. Its complexity is
+`O(C log C + n^3)`; over eight hard instances it solved 8/8, used at most 33,001
+counted Boolean operations, and averaged 0.000510 seconds. Plain DPLL with unit
+propagation also solved 8/8, but reached 445 search nodes and 808,167 literal
+operations. The compact route
+recognizes that the equation triples form one two-overlap cycle, decodes one
+right-hand side per quartet, and follows the cyclic recurrence. It solved 8/8 in
+298 exact bit operations. The benchmark tests recognition of that invariant,
 not resistance to software.
 
