    Given an instance $A_1,\dots, A_k$ of $r$-Tuple $k$-OV with $|A_1| = \dots = |A_r| = \frac{m}{n}$ (for some $n\leq m\leq n^2$) and $A_{r+1} = \dots = A_k = n$, let $V(G) = X_1\cup \dots \cup X_k \cup D$ where the set $X_i$ corresponds to the set $A_i$ and $D := [d]$ corresponds to the set of coordinates of vectors.
    
    For each vertex $x_i\in X_i$ add an edge between $x_i$ and $t\in D$ if and only if the corresponding vector $a_i$ satisfies $a_i[t] = 0$.
    Finally for each vertex $x_i\in X_i$ for $i\leq r$ and $x_j\in X_j$ for $j\neq i$ add an edge between $x_i$ and $x_j$.

    \begin{claim}
        The construction above yields a graph $G$ that has $r$-Tuple Dominating Set of size $k$ if and only if there are vectors $a_1\in A_1,\dots, a_k\in A_k$ satisfying $r$-Tuple $k$-OV condition.
    \end{claim}
    \begin{proof}
        Assume first that there are vectors $a_1\in A_1,\dots, a_k\in A_k$ satisfying $r$-Tuple $k$-OV condition. We claim that the corresponding vertices $x_1 \in X_1,\dots, x_k\in X_k$ form an $r$-Tuple Dominating Set.
        First observe that all vertices in $X_1\cup \dots \cup X_k$ are dominated by all of the vertices $x_1, \dots, x_r$.
        It remains to show that the vertices in $D$ are dominated. For any $t\in D$ there are vectors $a_{i_1},\dots, a_{i_r}$ with $i_1,\dots, i_r\in [k]$ satisfying $a_{i_1}[t] = \dots = a_{i_r}[t] = 0$ and hence $t$ is adjacent to all of the corresponding vertices $x_{i_1},\dots, x_{i_r}$ as desired.

        Conversely, assume $G$ admits an $r$-tuple dominating set $D$ of size $k$. 
        Without loss of generality, we can assume that for each $S\in {k\choose r}$ there are $k+1$ coordinates $t_1,\dots t_{k+1}$ such that any vector $a$ satisfies $a[t_1] = \dots = a[t_{k+1}] = 0$ if and only if $a\in A_i$ for some $i\in S$, otherwise $a[t_1] = \dots = a[t_{k+1}] = 1$.
