        And indeed any vertex $t\in D$ is adjacent to at least $r$ of the vertices from $x_1,\dots, x_k$, implying that the corresponding (at least) $r$ vectors have the corresponding entry equal to $0$.
    \end{proof}
    It remains to show that the constructed graph has $\bigO(n)$ many vertices and $\tOh(m)$ many edges.
    To this end, we may assume without loss of generality that $d = \log^2(n)$ and $G$ clearly consists of $r\frac{m}{n} + kn + d = \bigO(\frac{m}{n} + n) = \bigO(n)$ vertices and there are at most $d (r\frac{m}{n} + kn) + r\frac{m}{n} \cdot kn = \bigO(dn + m) = \tOh(m)$ edges.
\end{proof}

